from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long, render_note


def pilot_to_scale() -> str:
    body = '''<p>Most enterprise AI programs do not struggle because the first use case is impossible to build. The harder moment comes after something works. A team has a promising pilot, stakeholders have seen a demo, the technology has shown enough potential to create momentum — and then the question changes.</p><p>Can this become part of the way the organization actually operates?</p><p>That is a different test. The pilot tested possibility. Scale tests ownership, evidence, data, controls, workflow change and the ability to make the same kind of decision repeatedly without depending on the original project team.</p><blockquote>Technical success is evidence of possibility. Enterprise scale is evidence of operating capability.</blockquote><h2>The gap is usually organizational before it is technical</h2><p>Across large AI portfolios, I have repeatedly seen the same pattern: teams spend enormous energy proving that a model, assistant or automation can work, then discover that the operating questions were postponed. Who owns the outcome? What evidence is enough to justify broader use? Which data needs a named steward? What happens when the solution behaves differently in production? Who changes the workflow around it?</p><p>Those questions can feel secondary during experimentation because the objective is learning. At scale they become the work.</p><h2>The trade-off: structure too early, or structure too late</h2><p>Put too much governance and operating structure around early experiments and teams lose speed before they have learned enough. Leave every question until after the pilot and the organization creates a portfolio of attractive demonstrations with no reliable path to production.</p><p>I do not think the answer is a heavier process. It is staged evidence. As an initiative asks for more money, more users, more sensitive data or more influence over real decisions, the evidence expected from it should increase.</p><h2>Five questions I use before calling something scale-ready</h2><p><strong>1. Is there a real business owner?</strong><br>The person accountable for the affected outcome cannot be replaced by a project sponsor, technical lead or central AI team.</p><p><strong>2. Is the value mechanism credible?</strong><br>We should be able to explain which decision, workflow or behavior changes, how adoption will be observed and how that change connects to a business outcome.</p><p><strong>3. Can the data be owned at production standard?</strong><br>Access for a pilot is not the same as reliable ownership, semantics, quality and monitoring over time.</p><p><strong>4. Are controls proportionate to the real risk?</strong><br>The organization needs enough evidence, escalation and human oversight for the consequences of the use case.</p><p><strong>5. Does the operating model survive the project team?</strong><br>If the use case only works while the original enthusiasts are present, the organization has not scaled a capability. It has extended a pilot.</p><h2>The CoE should make this path easier, not own everything</h2><p>A central AI or Data team has an important role in creating common methods, patterns, guardrails and specialist support. The failure mode is becoming the permanent owner of every AI initiative.</p><p>My preference is a federated model with explicit thresholds: central expertise where common capability or higher risk warrants it; business ownership where value and workflow accountability belong; and a lifecycle that makes the hand-offs visible.</p><div class="rule"><strong>My rule: do not scale the demo. Scale the operating capability around it.</strong><p>When ownership, evidence, data accountability, controls and adoption are explicit, technology has somewhere durable to live.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Scale-readiness check</span><strong>Five questions before the next investment decision.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>Who owns the business outcome?</div><div>What value mechanism are we testing?</div><div>Who owns production data quality and semantics?</div><div>What evidence and controls match the risk?</div><div>Who operates this after the project team leaves?</div></div><p>This is the practical distinction I make between proving an AI use case and building an enterprise capability.</p>'''
    return render_long("Point of view · Enterprise AI", "Why Enterprise AI Often Stalls Between Pilot and Scale", "A pilot can prove that an AI idea works. It does not prove that the organization is ready to own it, govern it, adopt it or repeat the result at scale.", "17 Sep 2026 · 6 min read", body, aside)


def governance_accountability() -> str:
    return render_note("Decision note · AI Governance", "The AI governance gate I would never remove.", "Explicit human accountability before an AI use case moves into production-scale decisions.", "17 Sep 2026 · 1 min read", "Governance can be lighter. Accountability cannot disappear.", "A risk review can be simplified, automated or tiered. Documentation can change with the level of exposure. But before an AI use case influences a real business decision at scale, I want one thing to be unambiguous: a human owner for the outcome. Someone must know what the system is meant to influence, what evidence makes its use acceptable, what should be monitored and when the organization should stop or intervene. My rule is simple: if nobody can answer ‘who is accountable when this changes a real decision?’, the use case is not ready to scale.")
